## Slide 9

An Example:
Intrauterine Device (IUD) 

What is it?

IUDs are small T-shaped devices make to be inserted into the uterus to prevent pregnancy. 

Copper IUD:

Releases progestin, which causes thickening of the cervical muscles to block the passage of sperm cells.  

Creates a cytotoxic environment, killing sperm before they reach the fallopian tubes,

Hormonal IUD:

## Slide 10

Why IUD?

Remaining Problems

No systemic side effects
99.9% success avoiding pregnancy
Discomfort ends a few hours after insertion

Most women find insertion “severely painful” [4]
Pain management methods for gynecological procedures are not taught in OB/GYN rotations [12]
Little education on how to detect malpositioning 

## Slide 11

Lack of awareness and training surrounding Long Acting Reversible Contraception negatively impacts uptake and accessibility, particularly in rural, low-income areas.

## Slide 12

Solutions 

Resources for continued learning and practice of insertion and counseling skills for different types of birth control.
The Control Kit
Includes reusable models for practicing insertion and removal
Educational pamphlets for patient and provider
Competency checklists
Online Resource 
Scenario Practice consistent with updated eligibility guidelines
Addressing misconceptions
Comprehensive educational material
Online Forum

